    "items": [
      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-reference-order-two-state",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Two-state reference order",
        "reference": "research/physics-td-qft-rpm-62-2026-10-14-batch-3.notes.md#B-inventory",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound",
          "def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability",
          "def-tdmath-signed-comparison",
          "lem-tdmath-reference-order"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-comparison-and-stability": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-signed-comparison": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-reference-order": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $X=\\{a,b\\}$. For equal-mass nonnegative compounds $c,d$, define $c\\preceq d$ exactly when $c(b)\\le d(b)$. For signed unit-mass reference maps $R_\\lambda=(1-\\lambda)e_a+\\lambda e_b$, the order is strict from $a$ to $b$ and gives the full real-parameter reference ordering.",
        "strategy": "Proof strategy: direct\n\n**Given:** [A1] and $a,b$ as distinct tags.\n\n1.1 Reflexivity and transitivity follow from those properties of $\\le$ on the $b$ coefficient, and CH follows because $\\le$ is total. Adding a common compound adds the same $b$ coefficient to each side, so consistency holds. Multiplying both coefficients by $t>0$ preserves the inequality. Splitting a state coefficient into two positive parts preserves the total coefficient of $b$, so splitting/recombination gives equivalence. Finally $e_a\\preceq e_b$ because $0\\le1$, while $e_b\\npreceq e_a$ because $1\\nleq0$. [A1, F1]\n\n1.2 For stability, suppose $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\preceq B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for every $n$, with $\\epsilon_n\\to0$ and fixed positive $Z_0,Z_1$. If $A(b)>B(b)$, let $\\delta=A(b)-B(b)>0$ and $q:=|Z_0(b)-Z_1(b)|$. If $q=0$, every perturbed difference remains $\\delta>0$; if $q>0$, choose $n$ with $\\epsilon_n q<\\delta$. In either case some perturbed $b$-coefficient difference is positive, contradicting the assumed comparison. Thus $A(b)\\le B(b)$, which is $A\\preceq B$. [A1, F1]\n\n2.1 The reference pair $x_0=a,x_1=b$ is strict by step 1.1. Therefore [F2] applies and yields $R_\\lambda\\preceq_sR_\\mu$ iff $\\lambda\\le\\mu$ for all real $\\lambda,\\mu$, including parameters outside $[0,1]$. The signed parameters are handled by the signed comparison definition, not by treating $R_\\lambda$ as a nonnegative compound. [F2, step 1.1] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Lemma 2.2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "generation": {
          "role": "example"
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-common-meter-two-types",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Two types on one meter scale",
        "reference": "research/physics-td-qft-rpm-62-2026-10-14-batch-3.notes.md#B-inventory",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 9,
        "deps": [
          "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
          "thm-tdmath-common-meter-calibration"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-common-meter-calibration": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Take two types with fixed totals $T_1=T_2=1$ and a common meter satisfying the compatibility hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-common-meter-calibration]], including its shared equal-mass comparison $\\preceq_{\\mathcal Y}$. Let type-1 states $a_0,a_1$ have normalized values $0,1$, and type-2 states $b_0,b_1$ have normalized values $1/3,3$. Using the shared raw state tags of that theorem, let $C_0=e_{a_{1,a_1}}+e_{a_{2,b_0}}$ and $D_0=e_{a_{1,a_0}}+e_{a_{2,b_1}}$. Their fixed-meter encodings are $C=C_0+2e_{\\zeta_0}$ and $D=D_0+2e_{\\zeta_0}$, each of mass four. The calibrated values are $4/3$ for $C$ and $3$ for $D$; the common-meter comparison orders $C\\preceq_{\\mathcal Y}D$ and, after common-meter cancellation, $C_0\\preceq_{\\mathcal Y}D_0$.",
        "strategy": "Proof strategy: direct\n\n**Given:** [A1], [F1], and [F2].\n\n1.1 Both compounds have type-total vector $(1,1)$, so [F1] applies. Their calibrated values are $\\Sigma(C)=1+1/3=4/3$ and $\\Sigma(D)=0+3=3$. Since $4/3\\le3$, [F1] gives $C\\preceq D$. The value $3$ is permitted by [F2]; the representation theorem imposes no unit-interval bound on arbitrary states. [A1, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 Reversing the comparison would require $3\\le4/3$, which is false; hence this particular comparison is strict. No separate per-type numerical reading is treated as a proof of common-meter compatibility; that remains the stated hypothesis. [A1, F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §II.E",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "generation": {
          "role": "example"
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-reaction-offset-cycle-obstruction",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "A two-channel cycle obstructs reaction offsets",
        "reference": "research/physics-td-qft-rpm-62-2026-10-14-batch-3.notes.md#B-inventory",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 10,
        "deps": [
          "thm-tdmath-finite-inequality-alternative",
          "thm-tdmath-reaction-offset-feasibility"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-tdmath-finite-inequality-alternative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-reaction-offset-feasibility": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Set $A=\\{1,2\\}$ and $r=2$, with independently supplied data $\\nu_1=(1,-1)$, $d_1=-2$, $\\nu_2=(-1,1)$, and $d_2=1$. No offset $b\\in\\mathbb R^2$ satisfies both $b\\cdot\\nu_a+d_a\\ge0$.",
        "strategy": "Proof strategy: direct\n\n**Given:** [A1]–[F2].\n\n1.1 The multiplier $\\lambda=(1,1)$ is nonnegative and satisfies $\\lambda_1\\nu_1+\\lambda_2\\nu_2=(1,-1)+(-1,1)=(0,0)$. But $\\lambda_1d_1+\\lambda_2d_2=-2+1=-1<0$, violating the necessary condition in [F1]; therefore no offset exists. [A1, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 Directly, the two inequalities would require $b_1-b_2\\ge2$ and $-b_1+b_2\\ge-1$, equivalently $b_1-b_2\\le1$. Adding the original inequalities gives $0\\ge1$, a contradiction. This is the positive-right-side zero-left certificate of [F2]. [A1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Stanford EE464, Fourier–Motzkin Elimination lecture notes",
              "url": "https://lall.stanford.edu/ee464/lectures/fourier_motzkin.pdf?download=1"
            }
          ]
        },
        "generation": {
          "role": "example"
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-no-sinks-sandwich-on-r",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "A no-sinks sandwich on the real line",
        "reference": "research/physics-td-qft-rpm-62-2026-10-14-batch-3.notes.md#B-inventory",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks and independent review remain pending.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-complete-ordered-field",
          "thm-tdmath-infinite-no-sinks-sandwich"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-complete-ordered-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-infinite-no-sinks-sandwich": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "On $V=\\mathbb R$, let $p(x):=\\max\\{x,0\\}$ and $L(x):=x/2$. Then $p$ is an everywhere-finite no-sinks sublinear function and $L$ satisfies $-p(-x)\\le L(x)\\le p(x)$ for every real $x$.",
        "strategy": "Proof strategy: direct\n\n**Given:** [A1] and [F1].\n\n1.1 For $x=0$, $p(x)=0$. For $t>0$, $p(tx)=\\max\\{tx,0\\}=t\\max\\{x,0\\}=tp(x)$. Also $p(x+y)\\le p(x)+p(y)$ because the right side is nonnegative and at least $x+y$. Every value is finite, so $p(x)<+\\infty$ iff $p(-x)<+\\infty$. Thus all assumptions in [F1] hold. [A1, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 The map $L(x)=x/2$ is real-linear. If $x\\ge0$, then $p(x)=x$, $p(-x)=0$, and $0\\le x/2\\le x$. If $x\\le0$, then $p(x)=0$, $p(-x)=-x$, and $x\\le x/2\\le0$. These two cases include $x=0$ and give $-p(-x)\\le L(x)\\le p(x)$ for all $x$. [A1, F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "MIT 18.102, Introduction to Functional Analysis, complete lecture notes",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-102-introduction-to-functional-analysis-spring-2021/8fb8d5c170f1613151aca71de21027bc_MIT18_102s21_full_lec.pdf?download=1"
            }
          ]
        },
        "generation": {
          "role": "example"
        },
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
